\noindent These examples illustrate that the failure modes identified by the diagnostic framework are not statistical abstractions but manifest in individual instances. The LP and consensus failures frequently co-occur (e.g., ``Cramer'' instance), confirming that the signals share common blind spots rather than providing independent error detection.

\section{Practical Recommendations}\label{app:recommendations}

Measuring degeneracy and LP-F1 alignment at $N{=}8$ suffices: if ${>}50$\% degenerate, scaling provides no benefit; if degeneracy ${<}20$\% with $\rho{>}0.70$, construction may yield gains; otherwise prefer greedy.

\section{Use of AI Assistants}
\label{app:ai-assistants}

We used Claude (Anthropic) as an AI coding and writing assistant
throughout this work. Specifically:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Code:} drafting experiment scripts, data-processing
    pipelines, and analysis utilities; debugging and refactoring.
    \item \textbf{Writing:} drafting and revising paper sections,
    polishing prose, and reformatting LaTeX.
    \item \textbf{Literature:} summarizing related papers to inform
    related-work section.
\end{itemize}

\multicolumn{6}{l}{\textit{NER AUROC (mean $\pm\sigma$)}} \\
Qwen & .579${\pm}$.008 & .600${\pm}$.016 & .659${\pm}$.016 & .677${\pm}$.011 & .631${\pm}$.013 \\
LLaMA & .565${\pm}$.008 & .593${\pm}$.006 & .632${\pm}$.012 & .673${\pm}$.009 & .606${\pm}$.005 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
}
\end{table}

\section{Training Configuration}\label{app:training}

Both models are fine-tuned with identical hyperparameters (Table~\ref{tab:training}) to ensure comparisons across model families reflect model differences rather than training confounds.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Training configuration. Both Qwen3-8B and LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct use identical settings unless noted.}
\label{tab:training}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
Parameter & Value \\
\midrule
LoRA rank / $\alpha$ & 32 / 64 \\
LoRA target & All linear modules \\
LoRA dropout & 0 \\
Optimizer & AdamW \\
Learning rate & 2e-4, cosine schedule \\
Warmup ratio & 0.1 \\
Effective batch size & 16 (2 $\times$ grad.\ accum.\ 8) \\
Epochs & 5 \\
Max sequence length & 2048 \\
Precision & bf16 \\
Gradient checkpointing & Enabled \\
Max gradient norm & 1.0 \\
Training seed & 42 \\
Framework & LLaMA Factory \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{2}{l}{\textit{Dataset-specific}} \\
SciERC split & 1861 / 275 / 551 \\
CoNLL / WNUT & +\,load\_best\_model\_at\_end, \\
& eval\_steps${=}$200, save\_limit${=}$5 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Prompt template.}
For NER, we use a structured prompt:
\begin{quote}
\small
\texttt{System:} You are an expert in Named Entity Recognition.\\
\texttt{User:} Extract all named entities from the following text. Output as JSON.\\
Text: \texttt{\{input\_text\}}\\
Entity types: \texttt{\{schema\_types\}}
\end{quote}
RE follows the same structure with ``Relation Extraction'' and relation type lists substituted accordingly.

\paragraph{Constrained decoding.}
Inference uses vLLM with XGrammar-based JSON schema enforcement \cite{dong2024xgrammar, willard2023guided}, which constrains outputs to valid entity/relation structures matching the target schema.
This eliminates format errors but may compress per-sample diversity by restricting the output space.\footnote{SciERC training used \texttt{enable\_thinking=True} (LLaMA Factory 0.9.4 default), injecting empty \texttt{<think></think>} blocks (4--5 tokens) into each training response with loss computation; CoNLL and WNUT explicitly set \texttt{enable\_thinking=False}. Constrained decoding at inference time enforces JSON-only output, suppressing thinking tokens. See \S\ref{sec:setup} and Limitations for discussion of this confound.}

